digraph ReasoningTree {
  rankdir="TB";
  "N11" [label="(5,0,0) The resurgence of common respiratory viruses did not occur simultaneously."];
  "N12" [label="(6,0,0) Induction-case: In New South Wales, Australia (January 2022 report): all respiratory viruses had lower activity in 2020; RV and AdV were least affected; RSV resurged September 2020; PIV and MPV had limited activity until mid-2021; IV remained very low by end of 2021."];
  "N2" [label="(2,0,0) Common respiratory viruses (IV, RSV, PIV, MPV, sCoV, RV) have distinct seasonal circulating patterns with global variations by geographical and environmental characteristics."];
  "N21" [label="(8,3,0) Abduction-knowledge: Understanding whether resurgence patterns are regionally specific or globally consistent is crucial for enhancing future pandemic preparedness and informing public health strategies."];
  "N22" [label="(0,0,0) Abduction-reasoning: We observe uncertainty about resurgence patterns (phenomenon), and understanding these patterns is crucial for public health (knowledge), therefore we need systematic investigation to determine if patterns are global or regional."];
  "N23" [label="(2,2,3) Abduction-knowledge: Global reporting of respiratory virus activity is needed to inform public health strategies and programs."];
  "N25" [label="(9,0,0) Deduction-case: We compiled a large dataset on respiratory virus activity across the globe from published studies, surveillance reports, and international collaborators."];
  "N28" [label="(0,0,0) Deduction-rule: If we want to compare timing of resurgence across different viruses and regions with varying data formats, then we need a standardized method to identify epidemic onsets and peaks."];
  "N29" [label="(13,0,0) Deduction-case: Epidemic onset was defined using a '4-week trend' method, where each week's viral activity was compared to that of four weeks prior."];
  "N3" [label="(3,0,0) In 2020, the COVID-19 pandemic onset and large-scale NPI implementation were associated with precipitous impact on common respiratory virus circulation; IV and RSV activity remained remarkably lower during usual circulating seasons in multiple countries."];
  "N33" [label="(0,0,0) Deduction-rule: If we have standardized epidemic timing data across multiple viruses and regions, then we can use statistical modeling to synthesize time intervals and determine whether resurgence patterns are globally consistent."];
  "N34" [label="(11,0,0) Deduction-case: We used a generalised linear mixed-effects model (GLMM) framework to synthesise the time interval between epidemic onsets and between epidemic peaks of different respiratory viruses during the COVID-19 pandemic."];
  "N4" [label="(3,5,4) Deduction-rule: If COVID-19 pandemic public health measures are implemented, then seasonal influenza epidemic may be extinguished with just sporadic detections."];
  "N7" [label="(4,0,0) The subsequent relaxation of NPIs resulted in unprecedented out-of-season RSV resurgence in several countries, likely due to depleted population immunity."];
  "N9" [label="(4,8,11) Abduction-knowledge: Full reopening of schools is associated with increased risk for RSV rebound (HR=23.29), and there is an increasing trend in risk for RSV rebound over time, highlighting the role of increased population susceptibility."];
  "NROOT" [label="(0,0,0) This study investigates whether the post-COVID-19 resurgence of common respiratory viruses was globally consistent or region-specific by using worldwide surveillance data, defining epidemic onsets with a unified 4-week trend method, and applying generalized linear mixed-effects models to compare epidemic onset and peak intervals across viruses and settings.", fillcolor="lightyellow", style="filled"];
  "NROOT_COMMON" [label="(0,0,0) When post-pandemic respiratory-virus resurgence is asynchronous and potentially varies by virus and region, a study can investigate global versus regional consistency by compiling worldwide surveillance data, applying a unified epidemic-onset method, and statistically comparing epidemic timing intervals."];
  "R1" [label="(0,0,0) The observed out-of-season RSV resurgence after relaxation of NPIs is plausibly related to post-intervention changes such as school reopening and increased rebound risk, but the provided premises do not by themselves prove that depleted immunity caused the resurgence."];
  "R10" [label="(0,0,0) The need to systematically determine whether respiratory-virus resurgence patterns are global or regional supports compiling and analyzing globally reported respiratory-virus activity data."];
  "R11" [label="(0,0,0) For comparing epidemic timing across heterogeneous respiratory-virus data sources, the study used a standardized 4-week trend method to identify epidemic onsets."];
  "R12" [label="(0,0,0) A compiled global respiratory-virus dataset drawn from published studies, surveillance reports, and collaborators requires standardized epidemic-timing methods to compare resurgence timing across sources."];
  "R13" [label="(0,0,0) Given standardized epidemic-timing data, the study used a generalized linear mixed-effects model framework to synthesize intervals between epidemic onsets and between epidemic peaks of different respiratory viruses during the COVID-19 pandemic period."];
  "R14" [label="(0,0,0) The standardized 4-week trend approach provides a basis for deriving comparable epidemic-onset timing information from heterogeneous respiratory-virus data sources."];
  "R2" [label="(0,0,0) Country-level reports from Australia, China, and Finland support the view that post-NPI respiratory-virus resurgence was asynchronous across viruses and settings, motivating broader evaluation rather than proving a universal global pattern."];
  "R4" [label="(0,0,0) Because systematic investigation of global-versus-regional resurgence patterns is needed and global reporting of respiratory-virus activity informs public-health strategy, compiling and analyzing global respiratory-virus activity data is a justified study approach."];
  "R5" [label="(0,0,0) The COVID-19 pandemic period was associated with marked disruption and suppression of common respiratory-virus circulation, including influenza and RSV, against a background in which such viruses normally show distinct seasonal and geographical patterns."];
  "R6" [label="(0,0,0) The observed low influenza and RSV activity during usual circulation periods in 2020 is consistent with pandemic public-health measures suppressing seasonal respiratory-virus circulation."];
  "R7" [label="(0,0,0) Out-of-season RSV resurgence after NPI relaxation is a documented phenomenon that is relevant to assessing rebound risk under conditions such as school reopening."];
  "R9" [label="(0,0,0) Because common respiratory-virus resurgence did not occur simultaneously and understanding whether patterns are regional or globally consistent is important for preparedness, it remains a scientific question whether observed asynchronous resurgence is region-specific or globally consistent."];
  "N7" -> "R1" [label="abduction-phenomenon"];
  "N9" -> "R1" [label="abduction-knowledge"];
